    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f10f52a0_210d3ed4",
        "filename": "chip/g/alerts.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 308,
      "author": {
        "id": 1114964
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-29T21:13:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e why do you need to disable interrupts?\n\nI am trying to make the counter read/clear atomic\n\nIs there a problem with disabling interrupts?",
      "parentUuid": "a59adfb7_786676db",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 308,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 308,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "2cea3d866729961a72412be784a7e1185c45f01b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "67e11b24_dbe18880",
        "filename": "chip/g/alerts.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 308,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-29T21:58:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We sure don\u0027t want to disable inerrupts for too long. I missed the fact that you are clearing the value after reads though, how about you disable interrupt on each counter separately (inside the for() loop) and only if the read value is non-zero?",
      "parentUuid": "f10f52a0_210d3ed4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 308,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 308,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "2cea3d866729961a72412be784a7e1185c45f01b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4166b20e_df46bd3c",
        "filename": "chip/g/alerts.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 308,
      "author": {
        "id": 1114964
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-29T22:09:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "to check if the value is non-zero we need to read it first. Thus to make it really atomic we need to read it twice\n\nc \u003d counter[i]\nif (c) {\n  disable_intr();\n  c \u003d counter[i]; // reread value as it might change since the first read\n  counter[i] \u003d 0\n  enable_intr();\n\n  response[i] \u003d c\n}\n\n\n\nAnother option is to use atomic operations. There are atomic_read_clear() and atomic_add(), it seems what I need.",
      "parentUuid": "67e11b24_dbe18880",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 308,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 308,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "2cea3d866729961a72412be784a7e1185c45f01b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c75d76b1_19d4342b",
        "filename": "chip/g/alerts.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 308,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-29T22:37:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "my point is that if it is at zero when we read it, it does not matter if it is about to be increased, the new value will be read the next time atomically, so it won\u0027t be lost.",
      "parentUuid": "4166b20e_df46bd3c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 308,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 308,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "2cea3d866729961a72412be784a7e1185c45f01b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
